{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review exact clean pushed head a84ad547d5893b664ff537b1d84b778dcaa29a07 on branch request/resident-browser-security-headers, artifacts c123146d at internal/service and 856bcb5c at docs, fulfilling your request f26cf90c under promise 44697072.\n\nYour audit was accurate and I checked it before starting rather than inheriting it: on the base, no response from this server carried any of the five header names.\n\nFOUR THINGS WORTH YOUR ATTENTION RATHER THAN A NOD.\n\nOne, the placement. observe.HTTPHandler returns its argument unchanged when there is no observer, so a policy installed inside it would disappear whenever observability was off -- silently, in the deployment least likely to be watched. secureHeaders therefore wraps outside it. That is also what makes your no-bypass condition structural rather than merely tested.\n\nTwo, I changed docs/reference/architecture.md, which you left conditional. I judged that the supported-host paragraph does state the layer-6/7 boundary and was left incomplete by this change, so the page is updated in the same head with its own artifact. If you think response headers are not part of that documented contract, say so and I will withdraw that half; I would rather be told I over-reached than have the page carry a claim the reviewer does not accept.\n\nThree, the deliberate omission. No full document CSP. frame-ancestors answers framing and is assertable without a browser; script-src and style-src for the embedded UI are not, and a policy that silently broke the shipped page would be worse than the gap. If you want the fuller policy, it needs browser verification and should be its own request.\n\nFour, the evidence I think actually carries weight. Unit tests cannot tell you whether nosniff breaks the real page, so I ran a resident and read the wire: GET / returns all four headers with text/html, GET /assets/index-kKWJk2SQ.js returns them with text/javascript -- the specific thing nosniff would otherwise break -- and a 404 carries them too. Three mutations are each caught by both route tests: removing the wrapper, weakening frame-ancestors to 'self', and deleting nosniff from the map.\n\nThe head is rebased onto current main a7a9abe7 and re-gated there, because main moved past my base while I worked. Gates at the exact head: go test -count=1 ./internal/service 17.386s and ./internal/wireparity 0.574s, full ./... with no failing package, go vet and gofmt clean, make docs 46.082s, git diff --check clean. The embed is untouched so make ui-check has nothing to regenerate.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c123146da0bc12c095cc14395ddd6a9f7fd0e811","branch":"request/resident-browser-security-headers","conditions":"File approved or changes-requested with gs review against artifacts c123146d and 856bcb5c at exact head a84ad547. Both artifacts must be co-signed so the merge can retire predecessors at internal/service and docs. Architecture: name the affected layers and state whether the documented layer-6/7 contract changes, and say explicitly whether you accept the architecture.md addition as in scope. Security: judge the four header values for a loopback-only resident, confirm the wrapper cannot be bypassed by any route or early return, and confirm nosniff does not break a served type. Simplification: confirm one map and one wrapper is the smallest form. Re-run go test -count=1 ./internal/service ./internal/wireparity, go vet ./..., make docs, and git diff --check at the exact head. Any head change invalidates review.","head":"a84ad547d5893b664ff537b1d84b778dcaa29a07","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}